public final class ReachabilityExample {
    static Object retained;

    static void references() {
        Object first = new Object();
        Object alias = first;

        first = null; // the object is still reachable through alias
        retained = alias;
        alias = null; // the object is still reachable through retained

        retained = null; // now it may be eligible if no other path exists
    }

    static void unreachableCycle() {
        Node left = new Node();
        Node right = new Node();
        left.next = right;
        right.next = left;
        // If neither node escapes this call, the cycle can become unreachable.
    }

    static final class Node { Node next; }
}
```

These comments illustrate reference paths, not a guaranteed collection instant. The JIT may also prove that a local is no longer needed before the source scope ends.

```java
// Conceptual representation of mark-and-sweep
public class MarkSweepDemo {

    static class Node {
        String data;
        Node next;
        boolean marked = false; // GC mark bit

        Node(String data) { this.data = data; }
    }

    // Simulating the MARK phase
    static void markReachable(Node root) {
        if (root == null || root.marked) return;

        root.marked = true; // Mark this object as reachable
        markReachable(root.next); // Traverse to next
    }

    // Simulating the SWEEP phase
    static void sweepUnreachable(List<Node> heap) {
        heap.removeIf(node -> !node.marked);
        // In real GC, this reclaims memory
        // Unmarked objects are dead and removed
    }
}
```

```java
@RestController
public class OrderController {
    private final OrderService orderService;

    @GetMapping("/orders/{id}")
    public ResponseEntity<Order> getOrder(@PathVariable Long id) {
        long start = System.nanoTime();
        var order = orderService.findById(id);
        long elapsedNanos = System.nanoTime() - start;
        // elapsedNanos includes any STW interval that overlapped this work.
        return ResponseEntity.ok(order);
    }
}
```

Use a monotonic clock for elapsed time and correlate request telemetry with unified GC logs, for example `-Xlog:gc*:file=gc.log:time,uptime`. One slow request does not prove that GC caused it.

```java
@Service
public class ReportService {
    private final ReportRepository repository; // retained with the service

    public Report generateReport(long userId) {
        var records = repository.findByUserId(userId);
        var builder = new StringBuilder();
        for (var record : records) {
            builder.append(record).append('\n');
        }
        return new Report(builder.toString());
    }
}
```

The service and repository are usually long-lived, while `builder` is useful only during this call. That lifetime pattern illustrates why generational collection can help, but source code does not prove where any object is physically placed. Escape analysis, large-object rules, collector choice, and promotion policy can change the result. Use collector logs to observe the actual allocation and promotion behavior.

