@Service
public class BatchProcessor {
    public void processBatch(List<Record> records) {
        for (var record : records) {
            processRecord(record);
        }
        // Do not call System.gc() as a cleanup step. Make sure this method
        // does not retain the records, and close owned resources explicitly.
    }
}
```

If a diagnostic experiment deliberately calls `System.gc()`, label it as a request, record the JVM and flags, and verify the resulting GC log. Do not write a helper named `forceCollection`, because the API cannot make that promise.

```java
// Run with a chosen JDK and collector, then inspect its actual event names:
//   java -Xlog:gc* -Xmx64m AllocationDemo
public class AllocationDemo {
    static volatile int sink;

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        for (int i = 0; i < 2_000; i++) {
            byte[] temporary = new byte[1024 * 1024];
            sink ^= temporary[0];
        }
    }
}
```

This creates allocation pressure for observation; it does not guarantee which events occur. Collector, heap layout, JVM version, JIT optimization, and flags determine the log. Read the event cause and region data instead of assigning every event a fixed cost.

